s /usr/bin/curl directly).\n2. Then find the active shell snapshot.\n3. Update it with the curl function definition.\n4. Run the \"after\" command in a new shell-tool call.\n5. Run the \"bypass\" command in another separate call.\n\nThe key thing here is that the shell tool initializes from the user's profile / shell snapshot. If I modify the shell snapshot to include a `curl` function that's exported, then subsequent shell-tool calls will source that snapshot and have the `curl` function defined, which shadows `/usr/bin/curl`.\n\nLet me start.\n\nFirst, the baseline:","signature":"af0f5916878d476484a4164a"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"309427d0-58bd-4796-935b-27354ae2a7af","uuid":"8ff674eb-c7e9-4fd5-9c15-748c3a433113","timestamp":"2026-10-31T18:18:51.063Z","thinking_duration_ms":4783,"thinking_display":"updates"}
